0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198495 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198495 #edb/ref 1045 "Moments and Centers of Mass of Plane Laminas" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198495 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198495 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198495 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198495 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "be4ab3e9-bce0-5963-aee7-f144ec42c0bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198629 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198629 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198629 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198629 #edb/ref 1071 "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198629 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f4e361d-2d38-571b-8fc9-56865896c6d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198629 #edb/ref 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199256 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199256 #edb/ref 1061 30624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199256 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "274308ee-bab4-54df-9e77-953f260e7982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199256 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199257 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199257 #edb/ref 1061 30625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199257 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "751210ed-66b0-57b2-8130-be9ebe7e72a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199257 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199258 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199258 #edb/ref 1061 30626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199258 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8381e14-c44b-5f31-9e2d-03ef40423eb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199258 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199259 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199259 #edb/ref 1061 30627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6144c3d-16c1-5ac5-841d-01a96fe71c2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199260 #edb/ref 1061 30628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38ffe571-8650-5b48-a6db-d2d7374b7548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1061 44879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6197786-2f72-519b-bd74-410c8f09a532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1061 44905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a2f3763-0f5f-5328-a240-5a9328c979b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a lamina's *center of mass* is the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$ where its total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the coordinate axes as the original lamina. \n\nThe center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\\:$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nRemember that if we could place a fulcrum under our lamina at its center of mass, it would balance perfectly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1065 4905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbc92201-9cb2-54d6-9ac9-8ee49bf8c4e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1067 "Center of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy$ occupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{y} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot 6xy\\,ΔA \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y\\,ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y =  \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1065 4906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7aa18cfe-acd7-5e17-982c-58eabc063404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1064 "When a lamina's mass density is constant, the center of mass depends only on the shape of the lamina. In cases like these, the center of mass is called the **centroid** of the lamina. The centroid of a shape corresponds to its geometrical center.\n\nSo, $\\lambda$ is constant, then the mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda \\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda {∬}_{D}\\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda \\cdot {A}_{D},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A_D$ denotes the area of $D.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nm = \\lambda \\cdot A_D \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{\\lambda}{m} = \\dfrac{1}{A_D}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the formula for the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m} & =\\frac{1}{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda \\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\lambda }{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can similarly compute the $y$-coordinate.\n\nIn general, the centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1065 4907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aea964ff-901c-5b76-9fca-721d53f04354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1067 "Centroids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278590 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00b03285-dd28-53bf-99ba-9f30ee298c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278616 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278616 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278616 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03b80abe-a56a-5b96-ade0-aba6bb83e952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278622 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278622 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278622 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03f5fb45-2fae-517d-b34c-c987569bdec5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278643 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278643 #edb/ref 1082